 { Boris Spassky – Robert James Fischer, Sveti Stefan/Belgrade (26) 1992 Learning objective: This game illustrates how White’s kingside expansion can totally suffocate Black’s forces. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 c5 3. d5 d6 4. Nc3 g6 5. e4 Bg7 6. Bd3 O-O 7. Nf3 Bg4 8. h3 Bxf3 9. Qxf3 Nbd7 { Planning the trade ...Ne5xd3. } ( { The alternative } 9... e6 { does not change the character of the game. For example: } 10. O-O exd5 11. exd5 Nfd7 $5 ( { or } 11... Nbd7 12. Qd1 $14 { transposing to the game } ) 
12. Qd1 Re8 13. f4 f5 14. Qf3 Na6 15. a3 Nc7 16. g4 $5 { ⩲ This is somewhat similar to the game. } ) 
10. Qd1 $1 { The game is heading toward a symmetric Benoni, hence trading pieces must be avoided to make use of the space advantage. } ( { Imprecise is } 10. O-O $6 Ne5 11. Qe2 Nxd3 12. Qxd3 a6 $11 { when Black has good counter-chances associated with the break ...b7-b5 . } ) 
e6 11. O-O exd5 12. exd5 $1 { ⩲ An appropriate decision. White has firm control of the e4-square and Black will soon run out of useful moves. } ( { Worse is } 12. cxd5 $6 a6 13. a4 Re8 14. Bg5 Qc7 { reaching the previous chapter’s structure. In this case White has nothing special, as none of his standard plans are strong in this position, while Black’s pieces are very well arranged. In addition, Black’s light-squared bishop, the least useful piece in the structure, is already off the board. } ) 
Ne8 13. Bd2 ( { The alternative } 13. Bf4 $5 { has pros and cons: it pressures the d6-pawn, but prevents the f2-f4 expansion. } ) 
Ne5 ( { A standard move is } 13... Nc7 $5 { intending to create queenside counterplay with ...b7-b5 , and now a possible continuation is } 14. Qb3 { temporarily preventing it. } ( { White should refrain from } 13... Ne5 13. a4 $6 { weakening the b4-square too soon, and after } f5 $1 { claiming space on the kingside, } 15. Qb3 Rb8 16. f4 $14 { Black is quite close to equality. } ) 
Ne5 ( 14... b5 $2 15. cxb5 $16 ) 
15. Be2 f5 16. f4 Nd7 17. Rae1 $14 { With a small advantage due to space. } ) 
14. Be2 f5 15. f4 { Gaining space on the kingside, and preventing Black’s counterplay with ...f5-f4. } Nf7 16. g4 $1 { White does not miss a chance to put pressure on Black’s kingside; his advantage is already evident. } Nh6 ( { The try } 16... Qh4 $6 { is simply met by } 17. Kg2 $16 { threatening g4-g5 trapping the queen. } ) 
 ( { If } 16... fxg4 17. hxg4 { White’s king is much safer than Black’s. For example, } Bd4 18. Kg2 Nc7 19. Rh1 Qf6 20. Ne4 Qg7 21. Qc2 $16 { where White has a significant advantage due to his superior space and coordination. } ) 
17. Kg2 Nc7 18. g5 Nf7 19. Rb1 { This move is rather unnecessary. } ( { It was better to develop the kingside initiative with } 19. h4 { and then: A: The break } b5 $2 { is premature } ( { B: } 19. Rb1 h5 $5 { This double-edged move might be necessary. } 20. gxh6 ( { or } 20. Bd3 $5 { ⩲ aiming to sacrifice a piece on the kingside by means of Ne2-g3-f5 or h5 } ) 
Nxh6 21. h5 gxh5 22. Bxh5 Bd4 23. Rh1 $16 { White is much better prepared to fight along the g- and h-files. } ) 
 ( { C: } 19. Rb1 a6 20. a4 Rb8 21. h5 $16 { White may play positionally with h5-h6, or tactically with Rh1 , } 22. Bd3 { and Qf3-h3. Black’s pieces are very restrained, making defence difficult. } ) 
 { due to } 20. cxb5 Rb8 21. a4 $16 { as there is no compensation for the pawn. } ) 
Re8 20. Bd3 Rb8 21. h4 a6 22. Qc2 ( { Also possible is } 22. a4 $5 { since the break } b5 $6 { does not work as well: } ( { Instead } 22. Qc2 a5 { with the idea of ...Na6-b4 is too slow, for example } 23. h5 Na6 24. Qf3 Nb4 25. Rh1 Nxd3 26. Qxd3 { followed by a decisive invasion down the h-file. } ) 
23. axb5 axb5 24. Nxb5 Nxb5 25. cxb5 $16 { Though Black’s chances of counterplay are better here than in the game. } ) 
b5 23. b3 Rb7 ( { Another option was } 23... Qd7 { planning to double rooks on the b-file. Ftacnik suggested the interesting variation: } 24. Ne2 ( 23... Rb7 23. h5 $5 { ⩲ } ) 
Rb7 25. Ng3 Reb8 26. Nxf5 $3 bxc4 ( 26... gxf5 27. Bxf5 Qd8 28. Bxh7 { and White’s attack is devastating } ) 
 { And now Black’s counterplay is repelled with: } 27. bxc4 Rb2 28. Ng3 $1 { ± } Rxc2 $2 29. Rxb8 Ne8 30. Bxc2 $18 ) 
24. Rbe1 { Trading a pair of rooks limits Black’s counterplay, while White’s attacking chances remain intact. } ( { The immediate } 24. h5 $5 { ± was strong too. } ) 
Rxe1 25. Rxe1 Qb8 ( { Preventing the expansion h4-h5-h6 with } 25... h5 $5 { allows } 26. Ne2 $1 { threatening ...Ng3xf5 or ...Bxf5 , and now } Qb8 27. Ng3 bxc4 28. Bxc4 Nb5 29. Qd3 $16 { leaves Black in a precarious position due to the weakened kingside and the lack of counterplay.^ } ) 
26. Bc1 { Covering the b2-square. } ( { Again } 26. h5 $5 { is possible. After } bxc4 27. Bxc4 $16 { White’s queenside remains rock solid while his kingside play keeps on rolling. } ) 
Qd8 ( 26... bxc4 { doesn’t help after } 27. bxc4 $16 { since Black does not have any targets down the b-file. } ) 
27. Ne2 bxc4 28. bxc4 { Note that Black has no entry points on the b-file. } ( { White is also better after } 28. Bxc4 $6 { but the d5-pawn is turned into a weakness unnecessarily. } ) 
Ne8 29. h5 Re7 30. h6 $1 { +- The game is positionally won and the rest is a beautiful example of Spassky’s technique. } ( { Another strong continuation was } 30. Rh1 Rb7 31. Ng1 $1 { planning Nf3-h4 pressuring the g6-pawn. Black’s position is about to collapse. For example: } Qe7 32. Nf3 Nd8 33. hxg6 hxg6 34. Nh4 Kf7 ( { or } 34. h6 Qf7 35. Be2 $1 { +- followed by Nxg6 } ) 
35. Qd1 $1 { +- And there is no good defence against the threat of Nxg6 . } ) 
Bh8 ( { Even worse is } 30... Bf8 $6 31. Bb2 { since White’s occupation of the long diagonal is deadly for Black. } ) 
31. Bd2 Rb7 32. Rb1 Qb8 33. Ng3 Rxb1 34. Qxb1 Qxb1 35. Bxb1 { Now White’s king will march to the queenside while Black’s king cannot easily abandon the kingside, due to the potential sacrifice Nxf5 followed by Bxf5 and Bxh7. } Bb2 36. Kf3 Kf8 37. Ke2 ( { Another option was: } 37. Nxf5 $5 gxf5 38. Bxf5 Kg8 39. Bc8 Nc7 40. Ba5 $18 ) 
Nh8 { Preparing ...Ke7. } ( { Centralizing the king at once with } 37... Ke7 $2 { fails to } 38. Nxf5 $1 gxf5 39. Bxf5 { followed by Bxh7 winning easily. } ) 
38. Kd1 Ke7 39. Kc2 Bd4 40. Kb3 { Black’s queenside is defenceless. } Bf2 ( 40... Kd7 $2 { runs into } 41. Nxf5 $1 gxf5 42. Bxf5 Ke7 43. Bxh7 { and the pawns quickly decide the game. } ) 
41. Nh1 $6 { This is unnecessary, but still winning. } ( { Easier was } 41. Nxf5 $1 gxf5 42. Bxf5 Kf7 ( { now there is no time for } 42. Nh1 Ng6 $2 43. Bxg6 hxg6 44. h7 ) 
43. Bxh7 { when the three passed pawns win easily. } ) 
Bh4 ( 41... Bd4 { doesn’t help due to } 42. Ka4 Kd7 43. Ka5 Nc7 44. Ng3 $1 { followed by Nxf5 winning. } ) 
42. Ka4 Nc7 43. Ka5 Kd7 44. Kb6 Kc8 45. Bc2 { The entrance of White’s bishops decides the game; the rest is simple. } Nf7 46. Ba4 Kb8 47. Bd7 Nd8 48. Bc3 $1 { Black is in zugzwang. } Na8+ 49. Kxa6 Nc7+ 50. Kb6 Na8+ 51. Ka5 Kb7 52. Kb5 Nc7+ 53. Ka4 Na8 54. Kb3 Kc7 55. Be8 Kc8 56. Bf6 Nc7 57. Bxg6 $1 hxg6 58. Bxd8 { Final remarks 1. I feel amazed every time I see this game. Black was positionally crushed, and it is not even obvious what his mistake was. 2. I believe Black’s biggest mistake was choosing to play this type of structure under unfavourable conditions. In recent years, White has scored over 80% from the position after 10. Qd1; this should tell us something. 3. Black’s biggest problem was being unable to prevent White’s expansion with g2-g4. In an ideal case, Black would have had a knight on f6 and a bishop on c8 preventing such an advance. 4. After White’s 18th move g4-g5, I cannot find any good suggestions for Black. His position is bad, and his defensive task near impossible to conduct. 5. As the reader may have noticed, the opening of the kingside often turned out to favour White. This is a characteristic inherited from the structure c4-d5 vs. c5-d6: this tiny spatial advantage makes all the difference. } 
 1-0

