 { Ivan Morovic – Mahmood Lodhi, Khanty-Mansiysk Olympiad 2010 Learning objective: This game illustrates how White may pressure Black’s vulnerable d6-pawn. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 c5 3. d5 d6 4. Nc3 g6 5. e4 Bg7 6. Nf3 O-O 7. h3 e6 8. Be2 ( { PH } 8. Bd3 { is more common, but only if White retakes with the c-pawn after } exd5 { . After } 9. exd5 Re8 10. Be3 Bh6 11. O-O Bxe3 12. fxe3 { gives White great play on the f-file. However, } Rxe3 $5 { is a very interesting exchange sac introduced by Morozevich which gives Black decent play. } ) 
exd5 9. exd5 Nbd7 $6 ( { More common is } 9... Re8 10. O-O Ne4 { trading off a piece to alleviate the space disadvantage. } 11. Nxe4 Rxe4 12. Bd3 Re8 13. Bg5 $14 { White’s position is preferable due to his space and lead in development. } ) 
 ( { Another option is } 9... Bf5 10. g4 Be4 11. Bf4 ( 10... Nbd7 10. O-O $5 ) 
Bxf3 12. Bxf3 Ne8 { with a double-edged position, as in Goloshchapov – Vaulin, Zadar 2000. PH: Instead of 10.g4, White could just castle } 10. O-O { , intending to answer } Ne4 { , trying to exchange pieces, with } 11. Nb1 $5 { , preparing Bd3 and Qc2, eventually pushing Black back. } ) 
10. O-O a6 11. Bf4 $1 { ⩲ Due to the inaccurate 10...Nbd7, the d6-pawn has become a weakness. } Qc7 12. Qd2 Re8 ( { If } 12... Ne8 { intending to follow up with ...Ne5 simplifying the position, then } 13. Rad1 $1 { leaves Black in an awkward position since } Ne5 $2 { is met by: } 14. Bxe5 dxe5 15. d6 Qd7 16. Rfe1 Rb8 17. Qd5 $18 ) 
13. Rfe1 Nb6 { A rather strange choice, but Black needs to complete his development. } ( { As usual, the break } 13... b5 $5 { gives Black some counterplay, though after } 14. cxb5 Ne4 15. Nxe4 Rxe4 16. bxa6 $14 { Black’s activity does not fully compensate for the pawn. } ) 
 ( { Trading pieces with } 13... Ne4 14. Nxe4 Rxe4 15. Bd3 Rxe1 16. Rxe1 Ne5 17. Nxe5 dxe5 18. Be3 $14 { gives White a lasting advantage due to the passed pawn on d5 . } ) 
 ( { No better is } 13... Nf8 $6 14. Bd3 Bd7 15. Ng5 $1 { followed by Nge4 with a big advantage. } ) 
14. Bd3 Bd7 15. Ng5 { White intends Nge4 to increase the pressure on the d6-pawn. } Rxe1+ $2 { After this serious mistake, Black’s position will suddenly be on the verge of collapse. } ( { A better defence was: } 15... Na4 $1 16. Nce4 Nxe4 17. Nxe4 Nxb2 $5 ( 17... Rxe4 $5 18. Rxe4 Nxb2 19. Rae1 $14 { leaves Black with some compensation for the exchange } ) 
18. Nxd6 Nxd3 19. Qxd3 Bxa1 20. Rxa1 { With the threat of Qc3 followed by Bh6 or Nf5 forcing Black to return the exchange: } Qa5 21. Nxe8 Rxe8 22. Be3 $14 ) 
16. Rxe1 Re8 ( { The computer-like defence } 16... Nh5 $5 { seems like the only resource for Black: } 17. Bh2 Rf8 18. Nce4 Nc8 { White has a big advantage, but there is no immediate way to capitalize upon it. } ) 
17. Nge4 Nxe4 18. Nxe4 Nc8 19. Bg5 $1 { ± The invasion on f6 will force Black to give up his dark-squared bishop. White’s advantage is nearly decisive. } f5 ( 19... Kh8 { does not help either, due to } 20. Bf6 $1 { threatening Bxg7 followed by Qc3: } Bxf6 21. Nxf6 Rxe1 22. Qxe1 Qd8 23. Qc3 { Black loses material by force. } ) 
20. Nf6+ Bxf6 21. Bxf6 Kf7 ( { Or } 21... Rxe1 22. Qxe1 Kf7 23. Bg5 $18 { threatening Qc3-f6 or h3-h4-h5. } ) 
22. Bg5 b5 23. Bh6 Qd8 $6 ( { The last resource was } 23... Rxe1 24. Qxe1 b4 { blocking lines to prevent White’s entrance, though Black still loses after } 25. a3 $1 a5 26. Bg5 $1 { threatening } b3 { followed by } 27. Qa1 ( { but note the imprecise } 25... Qd8 25. b3 $2 { is met by } Qd8 27. Qa1 Qf6 ) 
 ) 
24. Rxe8 Bxe8 25. Bg5 $1 Qd7 ( 25... Ne7 $6 { fails to } 26. Qc3 { followed by the crushing Qf6 . } ) 
 ( 25... Qc7 26. Qe1 { (planning Qe6) } Qd7 27. cxb5 $1 ( 26... Qd7 26. Qa5 $5 { transposes to the game } ) 
axb5 28. Bxb5 $1 Qxb5 29. Qe6 { And mate is unstoppable. } ) 
26. Qa5 { The white forces penetrate Black’s camp; the battle is decided. } Qb7 27. Qd8 Qd7 28. Qf6+ Kg8 29. cxb5 ( { The tempting } 29. Bh6 $2 { fails to } Qe7 $1 { forcing the trade of queens. } ) 
axb5 30. Bxb5 $1 { Decoying Black’s queen from the defence of the kingside. } Qxb5 31. Bh6 { Final remarks 1. White’s decision to attack the d6-pawn, instead of carrying out the kingside expansion from the previous two games, was well justified. White’s knight on f3 was an obstacle to such a plan, while Black’s 10...Nbd7 turned the d6-pawn into an immediate target. 2. The weakness of the d6-pawn was so serious it prevented Black from ever coordinating his forces. } 
