 { Giorgi Kacheishvili – Daniel Fernandez, Mesa 2009 Learning objective: This game illustrates how Black may gain counterplay by taking control of the key e4-square. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 c5 3. d5 g6 4. Nc3 Bg7 5. e4 d6 6. Bd3 O-O 7. Nge2 e6 8. h3 exd5 9. exd5 Nfd7 10. f4 f5 11. O-O Na6 12. Bd2 ( { It is slightly more accurate to play } 12. Be3 { in order to transfer the bishop to f2 . } Re8 13. Bf2 Nc7 14. Qc2 a6 15. a4 a5 16. Rae1 $14 { With a tiny advantage for White. } ) 
Nc7 { The knight transfer to c7 is part of the usual scheme, preparing the typical ...b7-b5 break. } 13. Qc2 Nf6 ( { An option was } 13... b5 $5 14. Nxb5 Nxb5 15. cxb5 Bb7 16. Nc3 ( { after } 15... Nf6 15. Rad1 Nb6 17. Nc3 Re8 { Black has good counterplay; the game is approximately level } ) 
