h5 $1 { ⩱ Highlighting the misplacement of White’s knight on g3. Black will proceed with ...h5-h4, followed by ...Bf5 with pressure. } 25. Bd1 h4 26. Ne2 Bf5 27. Bc3 $6 ( { A better option was } 27. Qb3 e3 28. Be1 Bf6 $15 { when it is not easy for Black to break through White’s defence. } ) 
e3 28. Qb2 Bxc3 29. Qxc3 ( { Or: } 29. Nxc3 Qf6 30. Be2 Qd4 $17 ) 
Qe4 $1 { ∓ The passed pawn on e3 becomes dangerous while White’s pieces are poorly coordinated. } 30. Bb3 Qd3 31. Re1 $6 { The decisive mistake in a very bad position. } ( { A better try was: } 31. Qxd3 Bxd3 32. Re1 Re4 33. g3 ( 33. Nc3 { doesn’t help: } Rxf4 34. Rxe3 Bxc4 $19 ) 
Bxc4 34. Nc3 Rd4 35. Bxc4 Rxc4 36. Rxe3 Kf8 { Black is practically a pawn up, and should win with good play. } ) 
Qd2 $1 { -+ White is completely tied up and Black finishes the job very nicely. } 32. Qc1 Bd3 33. Ng1 Qf2 34. Nf3 e2 35. Qb2 Re3 36. Qf6 Ne8 $1 { White’s queen has no real threats. } 37. Qd8 Kf8 38. Qxh4 Rxf3 { White finally had to resign. Final remarks 1. Black equalized rather easily after trading rooks on the e-file and transferring his remaining rook with 18...Qf8 followed by 19...Re8. This move was essential in fighting for the key e4-square. 2. And the control of the e4-square did in fact prove essential. As soon as Black occupied this square with 20...Ne4 the game turned around and White had to endure a difficult defence in attempting to hold the position. At this point, White was left without useful plans for the rest of the game. } 
