 { Yaroslav Zherebukh – Mauricio Flores Rios, Southwest Collegiate, USA 2013 Learning objectives: 1. This game is an example of how Black may carry out an effective ...b7-b5 sacrifice, in order to dispute White’s control of the game. 2. Something to note is how this sacrifice shifts the attention away from White’s positional plans. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. Bg5 Ne4 3. Bf4 c5 4. f3 Qa5+ 5. c3 Nf6 6. d5 Qb6 7. Bc1 e6 8. e4 exd5 9. exd5 Bd6 10. c4 { We have reached the thematic structure via a very unusual move order. } O-O { White does not seem to know what he is doing. He has not developed a single piece while Black has already castled. However, he has obtained a symmetric Benoni structure, which often gives White a stable centre and a lasting spatial advantage. I knew that if I allowed White to organize his pieces harmoniously, then I would be inferior despite my lead in development. } 11. Bd3 Qc7 12. Ne2 b5 $1 { I thought breaking on the queenside to undermine White’s coordination was my only way to avoid being squeezed as in the previous examples. I believe I was right about this evaluation. This pawn sacrifice manages to divert attention away from White’s plans due to the multiple threats. } ( { Instead the bishop is lost after } 12... Bxh2 $2 13. f4 { . } ) 
 ( { Natural development will lead Black into an inferior position. For example: } 12... Re8 $6 13. Nbc3 a6 ( { Again } 13... Bxh2 $2 { leads to trouble, now after } 14. Kf1 $1 { threatening f3-f4: } Be5 15. Nb5 $1 Qb6 16. f4 Bd4 17. Nexd4 cxd4 18. b4 $1 { +- Followed by c4-c5 or Bb2 with an overwhelming centre and kingside attack. } ) 
14. Bg5 Be7 15. O-O { ⩲ White is likely to take over the game as in the previous examples, most likely through a kingside expansion. } ) 
13. cxb5 { Accepting the challenge. } ( { Safer was } 13. Na3 $5 bxc4 14. Nxc4 Bb7 15. Nc3 Re8 16. Kf1 { with an unclear position. } ) 
c4 14. Bc2 Bc5 { The logical continuation. Black has excellent compensation due to the misplacement of White’s king and the weakened d5- and b5-pawns. } 15. Nbc3 $6 { This is a small inaccuracy, as it allows Black to arrange his pieces well with ...d7-d6 followed by ...Nbd7 and ...Re8. } ( { Somewhat better was: } 15. Bg5 Qe5 16. Qd2 ( { or } 16. Bxf6 $6 Qxf6 17. Nbc3 Re8 18. Kf1 d6 { when White’s position is rather delicate, for example } 19. Qe1 $4 Qxf3 $1 20. gxf3 Bh3 ) 
a6 $5 ( 16. Nbc3 Qxb2 $4 { loses to } 17. Bxh7 ) 
17. b6 $1 { With a dynamic balance where any result is possible, say after: } Nxd5 $5 18. Be4 Bb7 19. Nbc3 Nxc3 20. Bxb7 Nxe2 21. Qxe2 Qxg5 22. Bxa8 Bxb6 $13 ) 
d6 $15 { Now Black is able to arrange his pieces nicely with ...Nbd7, ...Re8 and ...Bb7 with a very comfortable position. } 16. Kf1 ( { The alternative } 16. Bg5 { is unsafe after } Nbd7 17. Qd2 a6 $1 { opening lines for a dangerous attack. For example: } 18. bxa6 Bxa6 19. Be3 Qb6 20. Nd4 Rfe8 21. O-O-O Rab8 $17 ) 
 ( { Trading off the annoying bishop with } 16. Na4 Re8 17. Nxc5 Qxc5 { leads to an inferior endgame after: } 18. Kf1 Nbd7 19. Qd4 Nxd5 $15 ) 
Nbd7 17. g4 $5 { Although White’s position is not ideal, the idea g2-g4 is still valid (even necessary) to claim some space on the kingside, and to connect the rooks with Kg2. } ( { It is no better to play } 17. Nd4 a6 18. bxa6 Rxa6 { when g2-g4 will be needed sooner or later, as White does not have much else to do. For example, } 19. Bg5 $6 Qb6 20. Ndb5 Re8 21. Qd2 Bb7 $17 { when White lacks a constructive plan and he still has not connected his rooks. } ) 
Bb7 $2 { A serious imprecision. } ( { Accurate was: } 17... Ne5 $1 18. Kg2 a6 19. bxa6 ( { Instead the optimistic } 18... Bb7 18. g5 $2 { fails due to: } Qd7 20. h3 ( 19... Bb7 19. gxf6 $4 Qh3 ) 
Nh5 21. Na4 f6 $1 { Bringing the rook into the attack. } 22. Nxc5 dxc5 23. Nf4 fxg5 $1 24. Nxh5 Nxf3 { Black’s attack is irresistible. } ) 
 ( { Also the natural } 18... Bb7 18. Ng3 $2 { runs into: } Nfxg4 $1 20. fxg4 Bxg4 $19 ) 
Rxa6 { Now Black has an excellent initiative for the pawn. White must be extra careful, for example: } 20. Ng3 $2 Nfxg4 $1 21. fxg4 Bxg4 22. Qd2 Bf3 23. Kf1 f5 $1 { Black has a crushing attack. } ) 
18. g5 Ne8 19. f4 $6 { White gains more space on the kingside, but this move is careless as it allows Black some tactical resources associated with the weakened a8-h1 diagonal. } ( { Correct was } 19. Kg2 $1 a6 ( 19. f4 Nb6 $2 { is bad due to } 20. a4 $1 { ± followed by a4-a5-a6 } ) 
20. bxa6 Rxa6 $13 { when chances are approximately level. } ) 
Nb6 { Black’s plan is to create pressure on the d5-pawn. } 20. Be4 $6 { Logical, but inaccurate. } ( { Better was } 20. Ng3 { since now } Qd7 { is met by } 21. Bf5 { with an unclear game. } ) 
 ( { Instead } 20. Ng3 a6 $5 { allows Black to retain his initiative, say after } 21. bxa6 Rxa6 22. Be4 Qd7 23. Kg2 f5 $1 24. Bf3 Nc7 $15 { when the d5-pawn is likely to be lost. } ) 
Qd7 21. Kg2 ( { During the game I was happy to realize that } 21. a4 $6 { with the idea of a4-a5-a6 suppressing Black’s counterplay, fails tactically to: } Nc7 22. a5 $2 Nbxd5 $1 { The weakening of the long diagonal now becomes evident. } 23. Nxd5 Nxd5 24. Bxd5 Qh3 $1 { The key move. } 25. Ke1 Qh4 26. Ng3 ( 26. Kf1 $4 Qf2 ) 
Rfe8 27. Kd2 ( 27. Kf1 $4 Qh3 28. Bg2 Bxg2 ) 
Bxd5 { And Black is winning. } ) 
Nc7 $1 { ∓ It becomes clear that the ...b7-b5 sacrifice has paid off, since the d5-pawn and the a8-h1 diagonal are very weak. Most importantly, White has been unable to arrange his pieces in harmony, as he achieved in the previous examples. } 22. Ng3 ( { It is just too late for } 22. a4 $6 { due to } Rae8 $1 23. a5 $2 Nbxd5 $1 { when everything falls apart. For example: } 24. Bxd5 Qg4 25. Ng3 Nxd5 $1 26. Qxg4 Nxf4 27. Kf1 Bg2 ) 
Rfe8 { With the idea of a potential ...Rxe4 followed by ...Nbxd5 with a strong initiative. } ( { Even stronger was } 22... Rae8 $1 { ∓ with the additional option of ...f7-f6 opening the f-file. } ) 
23. Bf3 ( 23. Re1 $2 { does not help: } Nbxd5 $1 { -+ } ) 
 ( { Or } 23. Rf1 Nxb5 24. Nxb5 Qxb5 { , when the threat of ...Rxe4 remains strong. For example, } 25. Qf3 Rxe4 26. Nxe4 Bxd5 { followed by ...Qc6 and ...Re8 winning. } ) 
 ( { An alternative was: } 23. Qf3 Nxb5 $17 ) 
Nxb5 $6 ( { I completely missed } 23... Bb4 $1 { followed by ...Bxc3 and ...Nbxd5 , which is simply winning. } ) 
24. Nxb5 Qxb5 25. a4 Qa5 26. Bd2 ( { If } 26. Nf5 $5 { then Black breaks through White’s position with: } c3 $1 { ∓ } ) 
Qa6 27. Bc3 $2 { A calculation error in time trouble. } ( { Defending the d5-pawn with } 27. Bc1 $6 { does not help because of } c3 $1 28. bxc3 Qc4 { followed by ...Bxd5 with a strong attack. } ) 
 ( { Accurate was } 27. b4 $1 cxb3 28. Qxb3 Qc4 29. Qxc4 Nxc4 $15 { when Black is better, though White has some chances to hold the game. } ) 
Re3 28. Re1 Nxd5 $1 ( { The key to } 28... Nxd5 { is that after } 29. Bxd5 Rd3 { Black regains the piece with a crushing attack. Black is winning, but the game was eventually drawn as White found good resources in mutual time pressure. Final remarks 1. White’s opening deserves careful examination. If Black had not chosen 8...b5, he would have been left without active plans, and his development lead would have been worthless. 2. Black’s ...b7-b5 pawn sacrifice was effective because the d5-pawn immediately became a target and because White’s king became vulnerable. Without these factors this sacrifice could have been a failure. 3. It is worth noticing how White never really managed to organize his forces towards anything other than protecting his weaknesses. And everything Black achieved was at the sole cost of a pawn! This means the ...b7-b5 sacrifice is often well justified as long as Black can seize immediate counterplay. } ) 

 0-1

