[Black "Magnus Carlsen vs. Loek van Wely"]
[Result "1-0"]

 { Magnus Carlsen – Loek van Wely, Wijk aan Zee 2013 Learning objective: This game is an example of Black’s plan ...Qb6, ...Bf5 and ...Ne4 which creates pressure along the h8-a1 and h7-b1 diagonals in order to capture the b2-pawn. } 1. c4 g6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Be2 O-O 6. Bg5 c5 7. d5 e6 8. Qd2 exd5 9. exd5 Qb6 10. Nf3 ( { PH: Of course the fearless computer plays } 10. O-O-O $5 { here. The reason being that White's attack on the kingside is probably coming faster. } ) 
Bf5 { Black plays in a logical way: he fights for the control of the e4-square, and will continue with ...Ne4 trading a piece to alleviate his space disadvantage. } 11. Nh4 Ne4 { Black’s only logical continuation. } ( { The natural } 11... Bg4 $2 { runs into the tactical trick: } 12. Nxg6 $1 ( { but not } 11... Ne4 11. Bxf6 $2 Bxf6 13. Bxg4 Bxh4 $11 ) 
hxg6 ( 12... fxg6 13. Bxf6 $16 ) 
13. Bxf6 Bxf6 14. Bxg4 Re8 15. Be2 { When Black does not have enough compensation for the pawn. } ) 
 ( { If } 11... Bc8 $6 12. O-O { ⩲ White has a stable advantage and may expand on the kingside as in previous examples. } ) 
12. Nxe4 Bxe4 { Note how the b2-pawn is lost. This sequence of moves is rather typical in this position, and constitutes one of Black’s main lines of counterplay. Was Carlsen unprepared against it? The answer is: } 13. f3 $1 Qxb2 { As a King’s Indian player I faced this pawn structure rather frequently, and I often got into trouble due to White’s kingside expansion. Later, I learned this plan in order to capture the b2-pawn, but to my surprise even those positions posed some problems for Black, and this game is an example. Then the question is: Why does this happen? The reason is that in most variations, the weakness of the d6- and b7-pawns is far more important than losing the b2-pawn, and the well-advanced d5-pawn turns the balance in White’s favour in most cases. } ( { If } 13... Bxb2 $2 14. Rd1 Bf5 15. Nxf5 gxf5 16. O-O { ± Black is a pawn up, but his kingside is in serious danger. } ) 
 ( { The correct continuation was } 13... h6 $1 14. Bxh6 Bxh6 15. Qxh6 Qxb2 16. O-O Bc2 $1 { when Black maintains equality } ( { but not } 16... Qxe2 $2 17. fxe4 { when White’s attack is just winning, for example: } Qxe4 18. Nf5 $1 gxf5 19. Qg5 Kh7 20. Rf4 { followed by Rh4 } ) 
 ) 
14. Rc1 Bf5 ( 14... Bf6 { does not help: } 15. Qxb2 Bxb2 16. fxe4 f6 17. Bd2 Bxc1 18. Bxc1 $16 { The two bishops are stronger than the rook and pawn. } ) 
 ( { Interesting was } 14... h6 $5 { diverting White’s bishop from the threat Be7xd6: } 15. Bxh6 Qxd2 16. Bxd2 Bf6 $1 { The key to Black’s idea } ( { of course } 16... Bf5 $2 { is just bad after } 17. Nxf5 gxf5 18. Bd3 $18 ) 
 { . } 17. g3 Bxh4 18. gxh4 Bf5 19. Bf4 $14 { But once again, the weakness of the d6-pawn makes White’s position preferable. } ) 
15. Nxf5 gxf5 16. Qxb2 Bxb2 17. Rb1 Bc3+ 18. Kd1 { Black now faces the difficulty I described above; he must give up either the d6-pawn or the b7-pawn. Unfortunately, he will be much worse in either case. } Re8 ( { The alternative was: } 18... b6 19. Rb3 $1 ( { the tempting } 18... Re8 18. Be7 $2 { fails tactically due to } Re8 20. Bxd6 Nc6 $1 { when the position becomes unclear since } 21. dxc6 $2 { runs into } Rad8 $15 ) 
Bd4 ( { or } 19... Be5 $6 20. f4 Bd4 21. Be7 $16 ) 
20. Be7 Re8 21. Bxd6 $16 ) 
19. Rxb7 $16 { Black’s weakness on f5 is crippling. White does not have any weaknesses and Carlsen now imposes his advantage brilliantly. } Na6 20. a3 Rab8 21. Rxb8 Nxb8 22. Bd3 Nd7 23. Kc2 Bd4 24. Rb1 Nb6 25. Bf4 { And again, the d6-pawn is vulnerable. } Be5 26. Re1 ( { Interesting was: } 26. Bxe5 $5 Rxe5 ( 26. Re1 dxe5 $6 27. Rb5 $18 ) 
27. a4 $1 { The key move. } Nxa4 28. Ra1 Nb6 29. Rxa7 $18 { And White will soon capture both the f5- and d6-pawns. } ) 
Kg7 27. Bg3 { Threatening f3-f4 winning the bishop. } Re7 28. f4 Bf6 29. Rxe7 Bxe7 30. Be1 h5 31. g3 Bf6 32. Kb3 Kg6 33. h3 { Black cannot avoid losing the f5-pawn due to g3-g4. After losing this pawn the endgame is lost, therefore Black resigned. This game is yet another illustration of White’s endgame potential in this structure. Final remarks The main message to be remembered from this game is how Black’s plan of capturing the b2-pawn can backfire as the b7- and d6-pawns become far more serious targets. } 
 1-0

